From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: sun50i: a733: unknown dies get the vendor's fallback set

What. sun60i_a733_efuse_xlate() returns speed index 1 instead of 0 for
every die it cannot act on: a zero bin field, a programmed second fuse
copy, a bin >= 32, or a bin no OPP in DT describes. In DT, bit 1 is the
vf0100 frequency set (arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opps-per-bin-frequency-sets)
at the vf0100 voltages (opp-microvolt-speed1,
arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opps-vendor-voltage-per-bin), which equal the
unnamed worst case at every point bin 1 runs.

Why. Index 0 selected every point, because every point carried BIT(0):
an unknown die ran up to 2002 MHz. The vendor's own fallback for an
unmatched fuse value is vf0100, which ends at 1612 / 1794 MHz
(Allwinner BSP cubie-aiot-v1.5.0, sun60iw2_nvmem_xlate(): index =
0x0100; cubie-aiot-v1.4.8 had 0x0000, the vf0000 column).

Where the vendor driver differs, and what this patch does:
  - a zero bin field selects vf0000 there. An unprogrammed fuse reads
    the same, and vf0000 clocks higher than vf0100, so a zero read
    takes the fallback here.
  - a programmed second copy (bits 23:16) is preferred there. It is
    zero on all three dies we can read, so its meaning is unverified;
    such a part takes the fallback here, as it took the worst case
    before.
  - a failed fuse read selects vf0100 there; here it fails the probe,
    unchanged.

If a DT ever lacks bit 1 on some CPU, the driver says so with pr_err;
an unknown die then gets no operating points and cpufreq does not
start, instead of running a ladder nobody described for it.

Bins 4 and 5 take the same path as before.

Placed after the DT patch in the series, so no tree exists where the
driver asks for bit 1 and the tables do not carry it.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0; sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem.o builds with W=1
and no warnings (6.18.54, native arm64). An independent review traced
every return path to index 1 and the OPP core's fallback to the unnamed
voltage. Pending on silicon: with the test kernel's a733_test_fuse, the
log line and the policies' top points for an unknown fuse word (0x700),
a zero bin (0x0) and a programmed second copy (0x10598); bin 5
unchanged without the override.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem.c b/drivers/cpufreq/sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem.c
index 528973467..70b311cd4 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem.c
@@ -33,14 +33,14 @@
  * wafer tester burned in; bits [23:16] hold a second copy.
  *
  * The bin value is used directly as the speed index, so an opp-supported-hw
- * bit position in DT *is* the fuse value: bin 0x04 -> BIT(4). Index 0 is the
- * worst-case column and is what an unknown die gets - see the comment on
- * sun60i_a733_efuse_xlate().
+ * bit position in DT *is* the fuse value: bin 0x04 -> BIT(4). Index 1 (the
+ * vendor's vf0100 frequency set) is what an unknown die gets - see the
+ * comment on sun60i_a733_efuse_xlate().
  */
 #define SUN60I_A733_NVMEM_MASK		0xff
 #define SUN60I_A733_NVMEM_SHIFT		8
 #define SUN60I_A733_NVMEM_BAK_SHIFT	16
-#define SUN60I_A733_SPEED_WORST_CASE	0
+#define SUN60I_A733_SPEED_FALLBACK	1
 
 static struct platform_device *cpufreq_dt_pdev, *sun50i_cpufreq_pdev;
 
@@ -153,7 +153,7 @@ static u32 sun50i_h616_efuse_xlate(u32 speedbin)
  * prop_name are set once for all CPUs, so a bin described only in the A76
  * table would leave the A55 policy with an empty OPP list. Requiring both
  * clusters to describe the bin turns that into a deterministic demotion to the
- * worst-case column instead of a cluster that silently loses cpufreq.
+ * fallback set instead of a cluster that silently loses cpufreq.
  *
  * Only the A733 xlate uses this. dt_has_supported_hw() below is deliberately
  * left alone, so nothing changes for the SoCs we cannot test.
@@ -201,42 +201,50 @@ static u32 sunxi_cpufreq_dt_speed_mask(void)
  * The A733 speed index is the raw fuse bin value, so DT alone decides which
  * bins exist - no vendor mapping table is encoded here.
  *
- * Index 0 is the worst-case column, and it is selected deterministically
- * whenever we do not have a bin we can act on:
+ * Index 1 is the fallback, and it is selected deterministically whenever we
+ * do not have a bin we can act on. In DT it is the vendor's vf0100 frequency
+ * set, which is also the vendor's own fallback for an unmatched fuse value
+ * (Allwinner BSP cubie-aiot-v1.5.0, sun60iw2_nvmem_xlate(): index 0x0100;
+ * cubie-aiot-v1.4.8 had 0x0000). It runs the vf0100 voltage column
+ * (opp-microvolt-speed1). The cases:
  *   - the fuse word reads 0 in bits [15:8]. An unprogrammed fuse and a part
- *     graded "bin 0" are indistinguishable from outside, so both get the
- *     worst case. (The vendor BSP has a separate table for a zero read; we
- *     refuse to guess which of the two situations we are in.)
+ *     graded "bin 0" are indistinguishable from outside. The vendor gives a
+ *     zero read the vf0000 table, which clocks higher than vf0100; we refuse
+ *     to guess which of the two situations we are in.
  *   - the bin does not fit a u32 bitmask (>= 32).
- *   - no OPP in DT claims that bin. A die we have no measured voltages for
- *     must never inherit a table that was measured on a better die.
+ *   - no OPP in DT claims that bin. A die we have no frequency set for must
+ *     never inherit the ladder of a better die.
  *   - the second fuse copy in bits [23:16] is programmed. The vendor BSP
  *     prefers that copy when it is non-zero; on all three chips we can read
- *     (the 8 GiB board, the 6 GiB board, a second 6 GiB board) it is zero, so that preference is an unverifiable
- *     hint and we do not act on it. A part that has it set is, to us, an
- *     unknown part.
+ *     it is zero, so that preference is an unverifiable hint and we do not
+ *     act on it. A part that has it set is, to us, an unknown part.
  */
 static u32 sun60i_a733_efuse_xlate(u32 speedbin)
 {
 	u32 bin = (speedbin >> SUN60I_A733_NVMEM_SHIFT) & SUN60I_A733_NVMEM_MASK;
 	u32 bak = (speedbin >> SUN60I_A733_NVMEM_BAK_SHIFT) & SUN60I_A733_NVMEM_MASK;
+	u32 mask = sunxi_cpufreq_dt_speed_mask();
+
+	if (!(mask & BIT(SUN60I_A733_SPEED_FALLBACK)))
+		pr_err("A733: the OPP tables do not describe the fallback speed index %u; an unknown die gets no operating points\n",
+		       SUN60I_A733_SPEED_FALLBACK);
 
 	if (bak) {
-		pr_warn("A733: second speed-bin fuse copy is programmed (0x%02x, primary 0x%02x); its meaning is unverified on this SoC, using the worst-case OPP column\n",
+		pr_warn("A733: second speed-bin fuse copy is programmed (0x%02x, primary 0x%02x); its meaning is unverified on this SoC, using the fallback OPP set\n",
 			bak, bin);
-		return SUN60I_A733_SPEED_WORST_CASE;
+		return SUN60I_A733_SPEED_FALLBACK;
 	}
 
 	if (!bin || bin >= BITS_PER_TYPE(u32)) {
-		pr_warn("A733: no usable speed bin in fuse word 0x%08x, using the worst-case OPP column\n",
+		pr_warn("A733: no usable speed bin in fuse word 0x%08x, using the fallback OPP set\n",
 			speedbin);
-		return SUN60I_A733_SPEED_WORST_CASE;
+		return SUN60I_A733_SPEED_FALLBACK;
 	}
 
-	if (!(sunxi_cpufreq_dt_speed_mask() & BIT(bin))) {
-		pr_warn("A733: speed bin %u has no operating points in DT, using the worst-case OPP column\n",
+	if (!(mask & BIT(bin))) {
+		pr_warn("A733: speed bin %u has no operating points in DT, using the fallback OPP set\n",
 			bin);
-		return SUN60I_A733_SPEED_WORST_CASE;
+		return SUN60I_A733_SPEED_FALLBACK;
 	}
 
 	pr_info("A733: speed bin %u (fuse word 0x%08x)\n", bin, speedbin);
